1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:08,900
Welcome to today's deep dive. Um, imagine you are managing a massive national school registry Oh, sure... which is a huge undertaking on its own.

2
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:09,320
Right

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,900
And hundreds of schools are sending you their student lists.

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,720
Some are, you know, properly formatted spreadsheets.

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:15,940
But others are PDFs.

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,860
Yeah, or even just photos of handwritten notes, and the dates are written in three completely different ways.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:22,640
Oh yeah. That happens all the time.

8
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:30,620
But if you just take all that raw information and put it straight into your central register, you immediately create records that are fragmented and, uh, duplicated.

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:31,520
Absolutely.

10
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:35,520
And when data is that inconsistent, nobody trusts it.

11
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:38,860
It ends up entirely siloed. Exactly. Yeah.

12
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:43,320
Or it becomes locked into one supplier, and it is incredibly costly to change later on.

13
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,900
Plus, having to get things manually rekeyed just wastes so much time.

14
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:48,920
It really does.

15
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:58,560
So that is our mission for today. We are exploring how to actually transform that messy data into trusted records, and we are looking at a specific five-tier data architecture to do it.

16
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:03,140
Yeah. Specifically, we're looking at a published flow by, um, UNICEF Giga.

17
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,960
It applies a really strict logic to handling complex school data.

18
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,420
So let's, uh, let's walk through that flow.

19
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:13,140
How do the first two steps actually get us out of this data mess?

20
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:16,320
Well, it starts at step one, which is the raw tier.

21
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,700
When a school's file arrives, we keep it exactly as it is.

22
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:23,540
Like wrong column names and all?

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,240
Wrong column names, weird data types, typos.

24
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:28,180
It all stays.

25
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:33,260
Wait, so i- if you're building this out, your first instinct is probably to just overwrite the bad data.

26
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:38,620
Why keep a messy raw tier taking up server space if it's, you know, full of errors?

27
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:39,840
Why not fix it immediately?

28
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:44,240
Because if you overwrite the data immediately, you completely destroy your audit trail.

29
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:45,400
Oh, I see.

30
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,620
Yeah. The raw tier has to remain a completely independent layer.

31
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,040
That way you can always prove exactly what a school originally sent.

32
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:53,660
Right. For accountability.

33
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:59,320
Exactly. If there's a dispute later about a missing student, you need that untouched historical record.

34
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:02,140
Okay, that makes sense. You need the original receipt.

35
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:04,200
So what happens after the raw tier?

36
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:06,520
Then it flows into step two, the bronze tier.

37
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:11,600
This is your first automated filter. Bronze runs strict rule-based checks.

38
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:12,840
What kind of checks?

39
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:20,300
Uh, well, for example, it maps the columns to standard names, and if it sees, say, the word Tuesday in a date of birth column-

40
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:21,660
Which is definitely an error...

41
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:25,540
right, it automatically drops that entire row into a failed table.

42
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:30,900
Faulty rows stop right there. Only the rows that pass go into a pass table.

43
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,460
Okay, but bronze only catches the obvious formatting errors, right?

44
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:40,200
What happens if a school submits a perfectly formatted spreadsheet, but the data itself is factually wrong?

45
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:41,340
Like what?

46
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:44,360
Uh, like claiming a five-year-old is in the 12th grade.

47
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:45,340
How do we catch that?

48
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:49,080
That is exactly why we need step three, which is staging.

49
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:51,500
This is where the human element comes in.

50
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,580
Ah, so it's not fully automated.

51
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:59,460
No, it can't be. A person with the right permissions physically reviews the passed rows from bronze.

52
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:01,560
They either approve or reject them.

53
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:03,660
So someone is actually looking at it.

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:08,040
Yes. That deliberate approval is an essential act of responsibility.

55
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:11,040
A, a computer program just cannot replace that.

56
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:13,360
Got it. And once it's approved?

57
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:17,580
Once approved, those records move to step four, the silver tier.

58
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,780
Only the verified human-approved rows are merged here.

59
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:22,360
Right.

60
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,480
And finally, we hit step five, which is gold.

61
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:29,060
The data is merged and then split into two things.

62
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:32,420
Which are? A master table holding the core records

63
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,320
and reference tables for lookup values. Okay.

64
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:42,440
Splitting it up this way makes the database incredibly fast to query when people in the ministry actually need to use it.

65
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,020
And this isn't just a theoretical model, is it?

66
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:45,820
Not at all.

67
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,020
Databricks uses a very similar pattern of step-by-step layer improvement,

68
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,660
and GovStack uses this in the reference architecture.

69
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:55,380
The reference architecture, right.

70
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,280
Yeah. They use linked databases with scheduled rule-based automation.

71
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:02,900
Let's ground this with a real-world test case from the sources.

72
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,680
They use the Progressa Learners program as an example.

73
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:11,940
Right, Progressa. If we feed their learner files into this system, how does a specific error actually travel through?

74
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:13,360
Can we trace the exact steps?

75
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:15,980
Yeah, let's trace it. I'll number them so it's clear.

76
00:04:16,779 --> 00:04:17,680
First, step one,

77
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:22,620
a seeded file of Progressa learners enters the raw tier exactly as sent.

78
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:26,300
Okay. Let's say one row has a phone number in the last name column.

79
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:27,620
Definitely a mistake.

80
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:27,940
Yep.

81
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:35,260
Second, step two, that row hits bronze, fails the automated format check, and stops dead in the failed table.

82
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:36,960
So it never gets past bronze.

83
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:37,520
Exactly.

84
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:41,820
Third, step three, a registry officer logs in at the staging tier.

85
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,400
They physically review the rows that did pass.

86
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:45,920
And if they spot something weird?

87
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:49,220
Like a highly suspicious duplicate ID, they reject it manually.

88
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:49,580
Right.

89
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:57,180
Fourth, step four, only the pristine approved records hit silver, and ultimately gold holds the trusted master registry.

90
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:05,500
So the golden rule for a true data trust is that you need those automated checks at bronze, but you absolutely must have deliberate human approval at staging

91
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,220
before anything reaches silver.

92
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,880
Exactly. Technology handles the scale, but humans have to handle the responsibility.

